diff --git a/docs/shared-collector.md b/docs/shared-collector.md
index 9044b287..68370e9b 100644
--- a/docs/shared-collector.md
+++ b/docs/shared-collector.md
@@ -14,6 +14,8 @@ Each subscription has its own input buffer, named timezone, history generation a
 
 The live monitor retains a rolling 48-hour input window so changing a viewer's day boundary does not rebuild discovery, parsing, performance or traffic state. Calendar totals use each view's day start, including offset transitions. History projections are per view; live performance baselines retain the existing host-local-calendar meaning and do not change with an SSH viewer's timezone. Physical traffic sampling and its baseline remain shared; viewing it does not resample the machine. Unknown rates, partial histories and offline gaps retain the existing v1 semantics.
 
+Daily history peaks are observed live, not derived from log aggregates. For each view, the sampler raises that view time zone's `peakToday` maximum per calendar day and the history worker persists it in the private cache's `peak_days(zone, day, peak)` table. A projection merges only the peaks stored for its own time zone; the unnamed local view uses the empty zone key. Days the daemon did not observe while running stay unknown (`null`); the collector does not fabricate or backfill them from logs. A local time-zone identity change clears the unnamed view's stored peaks with its other cached projections.
+
 Any connected subscriber or live/pending authorized lease keeps the daemon active. Closing one bridge's stdin removes only that subscriber. A confirmed lease survives the last SSH exit until expiry, explicit unsubscribe, replacement, terminal relay rejection or bounded invalid-snapshot failure. With no subscribers or leases the daemon exits after 30 seconds. Candidate leases awaiting their first successful publish expire after 75 seconds even when the SSH channel remains open; disconnecting the last waiter cancels an unconfirmed candidate.
 
 Detachment closes inherited SSH descriptors, redirects stdio to `/dev/null`, uses a readiness pipe, and ignores SIGHUP. This is not a guarantee against systemd `KillUserProcesses`, session cgroup destruction, container termination, host shutdown or account policy. Supported background operation requires that policy to allow detached same-user processes to survive the SSH session. No root service or linger setting is installed. A host policy that kills those processes requires operator action outside this feature; re-open SSH and inspect relay freshness rather than reporting continued collection.
